# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Valve Three Opens Before the Clap

**Story & Context:** At 160 BPM, Malik begins with two clipped kick clusters and one dry clap. Nia performs a repeated trumpet note whose breath onset falls before the second kick, valve click lands in the vacant sixteenth, and pitched arrival lands just after the clap. That Valve Triplet becomes the principal hook. Asha’s choir enters only with hard T and K consonants for four bars before opening into a sustained minor-second cluster. Ruben holds a low C organ pedal while Marcus sustains G and D above it. Midway through, a Piston Vacancy removes three programmed ghosts so the trumpet valves become the only inner-grid percussion.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Close-up of a numbered brass valve tag marked 3 beside a pressure gauge and dry trumpet piston, one red maintenance stamp on cream paper, fluorescent reflections on terrazzo. Original sparse lyric fragments: “valve three / before the clap / air first / mercy after pressure.”

## Track 2: Consonants in the Maintenance Hall

**Story & Context:** At 166 BPM, the EP’s most aggressive track uses choir articulation as the dominant rhythmic layer. Asha writes short original phrases whose consonants fall into empty juke subdivisions while vowels stretch over one-bar organ harmonies. Malik reduces claps so vocal attacks can replace them. Leah uses trombone slide impacts and stopped horn notes as Piston Vacancies. During the central Mercy Hold, the entire brass/choir ensemble sustains one suspended sonority for eight bars while drums cycle through increasingly fragmented patterns beneath it. Ruben changes only the organ bass note, transforming the same held upper notes from minor to dominant to unresolved suspension.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Empty tiled maintenance hall with phonetic syllables handwritten on a service ledger, trombone slide reflected in a steel locker, pressure lines painted on the wall. Original sparse lyric fragments: “say the hard part first / keep the vowel open / nobody leaves the room / pressure is not the same as force.”

## Track 3: Mercy Is a Low Note Held Too Long

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 154 BPM but feels slow because the organ and low brass move in long values. Malik programs the sparsest drum track on the EP, using isolated kick pairs, rimshots, and large silences. Marcus holds a tuba fifth while Ruben shifts the organ floor beneath it every four bars. Nia and Leah use soft breath attacks with very little pitch. Halfway through, Bell Cut removes all pitched brass for one bar; only valves, breath, choir consonants, and organ remain. In the final minute, drums disappear first, then choir vowels, then organ. The last sound is a trumpet inhalation followed by one quiet valve release with no played note.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Dark civic hall after shutdown, one pressure gauge at zero, organ console light still on, trumpet resting on a maintenance ledger, empty choir chairs in a semicircle. Original sparse lyric fragments: “hold the low note / longer than the room expects / pressure at zero / mercy is what remains.”

